{
  "dom_id": "step-q120235",
  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/19f3d391ff30338ae70f59f1cf1ad40922eb8146c2be0993253a99173db2925a.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{\u2032}=x(y-2)^{2}$\n- ${y}^{\u2032}=(x-2)^{2}y$\n- ${y}^{\u2032}=(x-2)y$",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/e337a67c316dbee9e9c73849f363a7b2c3e3cb485bfef8eb49e87c3e33b98cf1.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=2$ and $y=0,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=2$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into $4$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>0$ | $\u2198$\n\n$(-)$ | $\u2197$\n\n$(+)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $\u2197$\n\n$(+)$ | $\u2198$\n\n$(-)$ |\n|  | $x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{\u2032}=x(y-2)^{2}$ evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=2.$\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{\u2032}=(x-2)^{2}y$ evaluates to zero at $x=2$ and $y=0,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>0$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n\n- Equation III gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{\u2032}=(x-2)y$ evaluates to zero at $x=2$ and $y=0,$ and the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>0$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
